# Contributing to {{PROJECT_NAME}}

## Before you start

- Start from an up-to-date `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`; never develop or push directly on it.
- Keep one coherent concern in each branch and Pull Request.
- Do not commit credentials, generated output, local caches, large artifacts, or project-specific
  private data.

## Branches

Use a lowercase category and concise kebab-case topic:

```text
feat/add-search-api
fix/handle-client-cancel
docs/update-quick-start
test/cover-index-recovery
refactor/split-runtime-service
perf/reduce-query-allocation
build/pin-toolchain
ci/add-sanitizer-job
chore/refresh-maintainer-docs
```

`{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` is the only long-lived integration branch and must remain releasable. There is
no permanent `develop` branch. As the maintainer, keep at most {{WIP_LIMIT}} implementation topics
active at once.

Use Draft PRs for incomplete or blocked work. Review Drafts after {{DRAFT_REVIEW_DAYS}} days without
activity and close them after {{DRAFT_CLOSE_DAYS}} days unless a recorded reason justifies keeping
them. Close superseded PRs immediately with a link to their replacement.

Start work with:

```bash
git switch {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}
git pull --ff-only
git switch -c feat/concise-topic
```

Rebase only your own topic commits. If rewritten topic history must be published, use
`git push --force-with-lease`; never force push `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`.

For dependent work, prefer sequential PRs. If a stacked PR is necessary, target its immediate
parent, document the dependency, merge bottom-up, then retarget and update the remaining PR.

## Commits

Use Conventional Commits:

```text
<type>(<optional-scope>): <imperative summary>
```

Use `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `test`, `refactor`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`, or `chore`. Keep the summary in
English, imperative, lowercase, without a trailing period, and preferably within 72 characters.
Mark incompatible changes with `!` and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` body.

## Verification

For source, tests, dependencies, scripts, build configuration, or workflow changes, run:

```bash
{{QUALITY_COMMAND}}
```

For documentation-only changes, run:

```bash
{{DOCS_COMMAND}}
```

Verify every new local documentation link. Documentation PRs still emit `changes` and `required`,
but skip the language-specific quality jobs.

## Pull Requests

Mark a PR Ready only when its scope is complete, local verification passed, and it can merge
cleanly. Every PR must include:

1. A Conventional Commit title.
2. What changed and why.
3. Design choices and compatibility impact.
4. Exact verification commands and results.
5. Documentation and worklog updates when behavior or delivery state changes.
6. Release impact: `none`, `patch`, `minor`, or `major`.

All applicable CI jobs and the unique `required` check must pass. Resolve every review
conversation. Do not add unrelated cleanup merely to avoid opening a second PR.

## Merge policy

- Squash merge only; the PR title becomes the commit on `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`.
- Never merge Draft, conflicted, failing, or unresolved PRs.
- Merge a focused green PR promptly; do not batch unrelated work by date.
- Delete the topic branch after merge.
- Revert faulty published changes through a new PR; never rewrite public mainline history.

Frequent small merges are healthy when each PR is independently reviewable and keeps
`{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` releasable.

## Protected mainline

Repository settings require PRs, the current branch tip, the unique `required` status context,
resolved conversations, and linear history. They include administrators and forbid force pushes
and deletion.

Use zero approvals only for a genuine one-maintainer repository. Require at least one independent
approval when another maintainer is active.

## Releases

A merge is not automatically a release. Create a tag and Release only for a user-visible
capability, a compatible fix set, a completed milestone, or a planned maintenance release.
Documentation and CI-only PRs normally have release impact `none`.
